[ Moderator's note: There will be no more messages on this topic in this
  newsgroup.  If you want to discuss it, gnu.misc.discuss is probably the
  place.  Or alt.flame.  I will ignore all mail on this topic, but, for
  the record, I think RMS is his usual self, and not making any friends,
  whether he's right or wrong.  --liw ]

That was not a misspelling.  Richard Stallman has changed the name he
uses when compiling distributions for Linux because he's upset by the
stance some Linux distributors are taking wrt. the free software
community.  He wants to make users conscious of that fact that a large
portion of the software distributed under the Linux 'label' comes from
GNU and the Free Software Foundation.  Technically, only the kernel is
Linux and the rest of the software is dominated by ports from BSD and
GNU sources.  He posed the question to me: do we name operating
systems for the kernel or for the software that it supports?  I have
exchanged much e-mail with him on this topic including the possible
public relations problems he is experiencing in changing the target-os
name from linux to lignux.  I, too, thought this was a typo.

Yet, it may be presumptuous (and confusing) to distribute a patched
version of Emacs that changes the target-os name.  Consider
distributing a version of Beethoven's ninth symphony where some of the
words have been changed.  Legal?  Yes.  Appropriate?

This is not the correct place for a debate.  I post this message as a
clarification.

Marc Singer
elf@netcom.com

