-----BEGIN PGP SIGNED MESSAGE-----

[I am announcing this on behalf of the original author, Julian Smart
 (J.Smart@ed.ac.uk); a few Linux-specific remarks from myself are below.]

   =====                  Announcing wxWindows 1.66B                 =====
   =====  A Free C++ GUI toolkit for Motif, Open Look, Windows 3.1,  =====
   =====                  Windows 95 and Windows NT                  =====
   =====                           --o--                             =====
   =====           http://www.aiai.ed.ac.uk/~jacs/wxwin.html         =====

   wxWindows is a C++ framework for developing multi-platform, graphical
   applications from the same body of C++ code. Currently supporting
   Motif, Open Look and MS Windows, a Mac port is under development.
   A third-party port supports development using Xt [i.e. using only
   libXt and the Athena widgets as a base].

   It is not just `yet another GUI library', but has been in use and under
   development for more than three years.

 Main features

   +   Simple-to-use, object-oriented API
   +   Graphics calls include splines, polylines, rounded rectangles, etc.
   +   Simple programmatic panel/dialog item layout
   +   Support for menu bars and status lines
   +   Toolbar class
   +   Pens, brushes, fonts, icons, cursors, bitmaps
   +   Easy, object-oriented interprocess communication (DDE subset) under
       MS Windows and UNIX
   +   Encapsulated PostScript generation and MS Windows printing
   +   Print/preview framework
   +   Document/view/undo framework
   +   Memory-checking and debugging features
   +   ODBC subset support
   +   Virtually automatic MDI support under MS Windows
   +   Support for MS Windows printer and file selector common dialogs, with
       equivalents for UNIX
   +   Under MS Windows, support for copying metafiles to the clipboard
   +   Programmatic form facility for building form-like screens fast,
       with constraints on values
   +   Applications can access MS Windows Help or wxHelp help system
   +   All source, examples
   +   Reference manual in PostScript, wxHelp, WinHelp, HTML, RTF forms

 Additional features

   +   Simple-to-use Windows program installation utility for delivering
       your applications
   +   wxCLIPS: an interface to NASA's CLIPS, for rapid GUI prototyping
       and application extension
   +   wxPython: an IDE for rapid object-oriented GUI development
   +   Tex2RTF utility for maintaining online and printed manuals
   +   Tree layout, graph layout, charting and grid classes
   +   Contributed advanced editor/hypertext classes
   +   Dialog Editor
   +   Contributed GLCanvas OpenGL/Mesa class

 Where to get it

   wxWindows is currently available from the Artificial Intelligence
   Applications Institute by anonymous FTP.  (See below for WWW.)
 
   + FTP to one of:

       ftp.aiai.ed.ac.uk/pub/packages/wxwin    (UK)
       ftp.mapsy.nat.tu-bs.de/pub/mirror/wxwin (Germany)
       silr.ireste.fr/pub/mirror/wxwin         (France)

   + log on as `anonymous', and give your user ID as password

   + Change directory into /pub/packages/wxwin/1.66, (unix or mswin
     subdirectories), change transmission method to binary, and
     get wx166*.tgz (UNIX) and/or wx166_*.zip (PC).

   + Get install.txt.

   + For automated installation under UNIX, get wxinstal.

   + Both archives contain the source code for both platforms, but only
     the executable demos for the specific platform (UNIX and Windows
     respectively). The demos are available separately as demo.tar.Z
     and demo.zip.

   Alternatively, in the U.S. you can get the large files via WWW from

       http://www.raccoon.com/~pcraven/files.html

 Installation

   See install.txt and the installation script wxinstal.

 Documentation

   You can view the documentation for wxWindows on the AIAI World Wide Web
   server using a viewer such as Netscape Mosaic.
 
   See http://www.aiai.ed.ac.uk/~jacs/wxwin.html

   TeX sources for the documentation are included with the distribution,
   formatted PostScript is packaged seperately.

 Mailing list for users or potential users

   Send your subscription request to wxwin-users-request@aiai.ed.ac.uk.

 Binary archive

   There is a binary archive for the wxWindows library at
   ftp://ftp.nd.rl.ac.uk/pub/packages/wxwin/1.65/platforms/
   (which is not necessarily up to date with the latest version).

 Linux-specific information

   Fortunately, practically nothing in wxWindows is Linux-specific.
   You should have no trouble compiling the library either with
   Motif or with the Xview libraries.  Just make sure you have recent
   and consistent versions of your development packages installed.
   I would recommend ld.so-1.7.14, gcc-2.7.2, binutils-2.6.0.14,
   libc-5.3.12 and libg++-2.7.1.4 (only the `libstdc++' library is
   actually required with wxWindows, not libg++ itself).  A small
   last-minute change to the 1.66B source has caused Motif compilation
   to break, however.  If you're using Motif, look at
   src/x/wx_utils.cpp, line 1592, and change `#ifdef wx_motif' to
   `#if 0' (i.e. uncomment the whole `#if'-block at the end of the
   file) to fix this.

Good luck,

Wolfram.                                  (Gloger@lrz.uni-muenchen.de)


