Hi Linux'ers,

I released a first alpha test release of the LDDK package that can be found
on the Linux Lab Project's servers:

 ftp://ftp.llp.fu-berlin.de/pub/linux/LINUX-LAB/LDDK
 http://www.fu-berlin.de/~clausi/documents/software.html

LDDK currently only consists of the DDL to C compiler that generates a 
whole character device driver source-tree from a definition file written 
in DDL (Driver definition Language). Currently only a few basics are 
implemented:

   o Submodule drivers can be generated automatically 
    (one minor number per subdevice)
     
   o A simple but flexible debugging code is included with the
     generated drivers. 

   o ioctl() functions are generated from function definitions with
     common call conventions in kernel and in user space.
     the corresponding user-level library functions will be generated 
     automatically.

   o ddl2c generates the whole source tree including Makefiles,
     user-library sources and includes. 

   o The driver is generated as loadable module

TODO:

   - A common configuration mechanism, so one common config program could 
     be used for runtime configuration.

   - A common memory handling API, so DMA memory and mmaps could be 
     implemented within DDL.

   - Documentation (see below) and code-cleanup.

Since the code is in raw-alpha state don't expect too much documentation.
If you think this project is helpful for You and for other Linux Users
please send suggestions and criticism to

clausi@chemie.fu-berlin.de

If you want to contribute to the LLP or to LDDK please let me know.


cheers
clausi



