This just in from the mklinux-announce mailing list.

Vicki Brown <vlb@mklinux.apple.com> sez:


Some late-breaking news from the MkLinux WWW/FTP server crew:

  *   The MkLinux WWW/FTP server has been substantially reorganized:

        http://www.mklinux.apple.com/

          The FAQ has been divided into topics and expanded.  Assorted
          status pages (e.g., Errata, Install, Release, Updates) have
          been HTMLized and made available.

        ftp://mklinux.apple.com/

          The bin and src RPM files have been split up into individual
          packages, allowing far more selective retrieval.

  *   A kernel patch ("bigmem") has been released, allowing MkLinux to run
      on systems with more than 40 MB of RAM.

  *   Red Hat and Walnut Creek CDROM now mirror ftp://mklinux.apple.com/:

        ftp://ftp.cdrom.com/pub/linux/mklinux/MkLinux_DR1/ 
        ftp://ftp.redhat.com/mirrors/mklinux.apple.com/MkLinux_DR1/

      Because of the substantial changes to the MkLinux FTP server, it
      may take a while for these sites to catch up.

  *   The MkLinux-Intel mailing list is back on the air, along with the
      dozen or so lists announced by Michael Burg.  See the FAQ (on the
      MkLinux WWW server) for detailed information.

However -

  Although we've got space for 150 simultaneous ftp sessions, the performance
  of the server degrades rapidly with each one.  Many of these files are HUGE
  (especially the complete release).

  Apple has printed up several thousand DR1 CDs.  These are available from
  our distributor, Prime Time Freeware (http://www.ptf.com, info@ptf.com),
  for a nominal fee.  Please consider ordering a CD as an alternative to
  hours of ftp downloading!

  By using the MkLinux Developer's Release 1 CD-ROM, in conjunction with the
  updates on the server, we can ensure that everyone will be able to keep up
  to date.  (Pulling down hundreds of MB, in competition with dozens of other
  FTP users, is NOT generally a good use of resources! :-)

