I have created a boot/rootdisk with kernel 1.3.86 that includes
all of David Hinds' version 2.8.13 drivers (except for sram/flash 
memory cards).  Most importantly, this means you can boot from this 
disk and install linux via a pcmcia scsi adapter (previous pcmcia 
boot disks only supported the pcmcia ethernet cards).  

the disk image is available from 

ftp.biochem.ucl.ac.uk  in the directory  pub/linux


The disk requires 8mb RAM or more to boot, and also supports FAT,
VFAT, NFS, all the standard ethernet cards I could get into the 
kernel, and all the basic network tools (route, ifconfig, telnet,
ftp, etc.).


