I've just uploaded the first distribution of the fast, free and
fabulous 3d zbuffer rendering library 'lib3d-0.1.1.tar.gz' (with
teapot demo).

It will be found on sunsite.unc.edu in: pub/Linux/Incoming 
for a little while before it moves to its true home:
pub/Linux/libs/graphics

The design of the pipeline and database is strongly biased towards
software implementation, maintaining internally a hierarchy of models,
cameras and lights which are manipulated by the user through the api.
This is the data model employed by most of the current high
performance libraries for the commercial OS's.

This allows large scale optimization of the model structure
(recognition of shared vertices, normals, materials etc), the
application of techniques such as bounding-box culling as well as
caching of information from scene to scene.

Most of the compulsory optimizations are present, including:
   o Bounding box culling and clip plane selection.
   o Backface culling in object space.
   o Lighting calculations in object space.
   o Model optimization including shared vertex, shared normal and
shared
     material detection.
   o Culling and lighting per normal and per light rather than per
polygon.
   o 8+24bit Zbuffer which needs be cleared only once every 256 frames.
   o Dirty region support for swapBuffer(), clearBuffer() and
clearZBuffer().

The teapot demo makes 25k polys/second on my P100 in a dithered 8bit X
window.
SVGA support is currently by contribution only.

Here's the lsm:

