 traf Version 1.1, Copyright (C) 1996 Herman le Roux, University of Pretoria
=============================================================================


 WHAT IS traf ?  
================

traf is a network traffic monitor. traf is especially useful for network 
administrators, helping them with planning, and seeking out bottle necks in a 
network. It can also be used to check on the users of a network.

Install traf and use 

	#prompt#> traf -help 

for more information (See NOTES WHEN USING TRAF section 5 before running it ).


 WHERE IS traf?
================

Anonymous ftp: 

Source:	     ftp://ford.ee.up.ac.za/pub/archive/traf-1.1-linux.tar.gz
Binaries:    ftp://ford.ee.up.ac.za/pub/archive/traf-1.1-linux-binaries.tar.gz


WWW:

Source:      http://www.ee.up.ac.za/~herman
Binaries:    http://www.ee.up.ac.za/~herman
     
   ( In both cases follow the link: Publications and Releases )




  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
  The author of this software can be reached at:
  
  herman@suntiger.ee.up.ac.za
  
  or

  Herman le Roux
  Department of Electrical and Electronic Engineering
  University of Pretoria
  Pretoria 0002
  South Africa
  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~


 NOTES WHEN USING TRAF
=======================

1) When using the option -ia, not all of the IP addresses will be resolved to 
   Internet Addresses because the function gethostbyaddr() is used.

2) Rates and other values that contain any information about time and/or time
   frames may not be absolute values, but rather relative to other entries. 
   Thus the values displayed under Kb/second can therefore be far from the
   real thing. This doesn't mean that the entries under Kb/second are totally
   useless, in fact the information 'hidden' in the values relative to each 
   other still indicates which machines are busy and which are not. The reason
   for the inaccuracies is because a buffer is used to capture packets. Every 
   time the buffer is full, the statistics are computed. This means, that while
   computing the statistics, other packets may become available for capturing.

3) To execute traf, one needs root access.

4) The slower the machine traf is running on, the larger the error becomes for
   rates etc.

5) Open a xterm to execute traf in, and make it as big as possible because 
   traf's output is *BIG*.
   I'm using xterm -geometry 139x60 usually.
    
6) traf is a text based program, but I suggest running it in a xterm under
   X Windows because of the w.

7) If the option -ip is used some of the IP addresses may appear funny or
   even totally incorrect. The reason for this is that traf looks in a fixed
   position for IP addresses to save time. If packets with addresses in other
   positions are captured, the IP addresses shown by traf will be wrong.  

8) Use <control-c> to quit from traf.

9) traf works only with ethernets and devices are looked up by libpcap. This 
   means that one cannot use a commandline option to set the network device. 


 PLATFORMS 
===========

This package is known to work successfully on the following platforms with the
C-compilers indicated:

 1) 90 MHz Pentium (Intel) 32 MB RAM, Linux 1.2.0, gcc v2.5.8
 2) 40 MHz 486 (AMD) 8 MB RAM, Linux 1.2.3, gcc v2.5.8
 3) 120 MHz Pentium (Intel) 32 MB RAM, Linux 1.3.20, gcc v2.7.0, slackware v3.0
 4) 120 MHz Pentium (Intel) 32 MB RAM, Linux 1.2.13, gcc v2.7.2, slackware v3.0
 5) 100 MHz 486 DX4 16 MB RAM, Linux 1.2.13, gcc v2.6.3, slackware v2.3


